{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjUZCtA4w6EH14Yq2xP4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**We Fit In** is finished, and the audit the stop hook asked for is done. The hook was right that I hadn't checked line by line against the plan, and doing it found real gaps, all now fixed. The final build passes all 303 scripted tests, but no person has played it, so balance and feel were only checked with bots and screenshots.\n\n## What the audit found\nThe checklist with its evidence is in `PROGRESS.md`.\n\n| Area | Found and fixed |\n|---|---|\n| **Correction to my last message** | `npm install` would not have restored the tests: my version pin had silently failed, so it pulled a Playwright that needs browsers this machine doesn't have. It's now pinned to 1.63.0. |\n| **Plan steps I had skipped** | Wrote `tools/clip-sheet.mjs` and looked at all 34 clips. Ran `check.mjs` on the real page and on the model sheets (0 problems, 0 warnings after fixing five). Captured Holding Still at six Dot positions, and the body does show where the Dot is. |\n| **Bugs only found by looking** | Coach's line hid the controls during every activity intro. Ready ticks were clipped on the session card. The attract banner was cut off on phones. The \"7\" bubble hid Kevin. |\n| **Plan details** | Four sample lines weren't verbatim. Two line lists were never played. Menu rows broke the size rule and tab bars ignored the wheel. The ending had no `Timeline`. Four planned files and nine scenes' fingerprints were missing. |\n\nTwo of the hook's points didn't hold up:\n- **Placeholders:** the `void` hacks in the game code were already gone. A few remained in tests and tools; I removed those, and a search now finds none.\n- **Browsers:** this machine has three browser engines, not four, and all three were run. Firefox and WebKit have no WebGPU here, so they only show the failure message, as the plan expects.\n\n## What can't be checked here\nThere's no real controller, phone or anyone to listen, so those were tested with the stand-ins the brief specifies: a stub gamepad, a phone profile, and measured audio levels. WebGPU is required to play.\n\n## Where I departed from the plan\n- **Coconut Header:** solo is one wide lane with no classmates, because the plan says both.\n- **Vitamin Tray:** pills release 3 at a time, because all 8 at once wasn't really playable.\n- **Dev Tools:** the Balance and Games tabs scroll past the 8-row limit.\n\nTo re-run the tests, run `npm install` then `node tools/test.mjs`. Nothing is committed to git.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 23:07:41\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** fitness and balance minigame collection inside a light life-sim week (sports). Isometric on the island, fixed front-on for class activities, other fixed views per activity (behind, side-on, top-down-ish).\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight engine kit) with three.js r186 and Rapier as bundled in it; esbuild 0.28.2 for packaging; vanilla JavaScript modules\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel look (toon materials, 16-colour palette), with new models written as data for a spotless white island made of giant gym equipment\n- **Animation:** engine ClipDefs with THREE.AnimationMixer, plus scrubbed reps, Squash, SpringChain and additive lean layers\n- **Audio:** engine Song and SoundDef data plus a Web Audio formant voice, all code with no files. Gibberish voices per speaker. Music 10% by default.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, own card, 1–4 players; Family Hour is local on one screen\n- **Testing:** headless Node playthroughs with bots behind one test command; Chromium, Firefox and WebKit with Playwright on file://; gamepad stub, phone profile, OfflineAudioContext and AnalyserNode checks; the hub harness; screenshots at 1920×1080 only for what a script can't judge; no subagents for review\n- **Research:** read the kit guides and the hub README, searched API.md. The director's plan included engine and hub research and spikes.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 60. Scripts caught about 20 (a crash, a dead activity, ghost messages that never reached other players, a save field lost on reload, and balance). Screenshots caught about 35 that scripts could not see (avatars vanishing, a dial hiding Ounce, prompts under dialogue). A line-by-line audit against the plan caught the rest (sample lines not verbatim, menus over the row limit, no Timeline in the ending).\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** Five. Step Class avatars vanished from a bad ease name. Avatars folded into heaps from a lean that added up every frame. Vitamin Tray scored 0 for everyone because resting bodies slept through gravity changes. Step Class crashed on a mat colour. Online, ghosts never received their messages.\n3. **Most helpful test?** Running five bot skills through every activity. In one command it found a crash and a dead activity, and it gave every balance number I tuned against. The 1920×1080 contact sheets were a close second.\n4. **Least helpful test?** The WebKit and Firefox runs. Neither has WebGPU on this machine, so they only prove that the failure message shows.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** Headless in Node. The same game code, physics and mixers ran a full seven-day playthrough with bots in about a minute, so I could test the real game constantly. Models as data with `checkModel` came second.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. A ClipDef key takes its own ease names (`in`, `out`), not the tween names, and `outQuad` baked NaN with `validateClip` and `analyzeClip` both silent, so avatars vanished only when the clip played. I also hung a script awaiting a scene change in manual time, and lost Tray to Rapier bodies sleeping through a gravity change.\n3. **Most important thing you had to build?** The Dot and its driver seam: one lean model that a person, a pad, a bot or a ghost can all drive, used by every activity, attract, the tests and online. Close behind is the HUD kit: mixed-case text, discs, rings, menus and prompts.\n4. **Hardest part?** The HUD is only rects, text and sprites, with no circles or alpha. Dials are drawn as scanlines, and menu dimming borrows the transition uniform. Pads are also merged into one, so Family Hour reads `navigator.getGamepads` itself.\n5. **What did you change in the engine?** None.\n6. **One thing to change?** Make `validateClip` reject an unknown ease name. It would have saved the worst bug of the run, and it's a small change.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The verified recipes in the plan. Knowing which numbers and calls were already proven meant I could spend the effort on the game, and the bot table let me check the core feel at once.\n2. **What was confusing?** Coconut Header is listed for lane staging with classmates on side mats, but also gets a wider lane when solo; I made solo one wide lane. Bias values such as +.10 have no stated unit; I treated them as stick lean, multiplied by authority.\n3. **Hardest thing to do?** Keeping every screen readable at 480×270 with pad, keys and mouse all working, while a dozen activities share one HUD. Composition took several rounds of screenshots.\n4. **What would you change?** Say whether Header solo has classmates, and give the unit for bias. Ask for contact sheets of every screen as a step, since they caught more than I expected.\n\nFiles are in `work/`:\n- `WeFitIn-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-230741.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `WeFitIn-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-230741/` (dev folder)\n- `PROGRESS.md`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":464,"cache_read_input_tokens":846504,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":464},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2ae2eb7-ae7f-44a8-ac16-77fd998e0107","uuid":"a957afa9-1532-4c6e-88c6-6ebf2d7c5778","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:36:38.262Z","request_id":"req_011CgjUZCPebtDvVDbaCi3zs"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not satisfied. The transcript shows the deliverables exist (single html and exit-interview.json in root, dev folder with the engine, 303/303 test pass). But the stop condition requires everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer to be done, and the transcript leaves several plan items unfinished or unverified. PLAN 19 build step 3 asked for a stop to judge Holding Still's feel with a captured run; the agent captured six static Dot positions, not a playthrough judgment. PLAN 9/20 required the About screen to hold feedback and CREDITS.md; the transcript shows pages rendered but no full scroll check of CREDITS.md beyond a partial test. Browser coverage is two engines exercised fully (Chromium) and two only for the no-WebGPU failure message, with WebKit iPhone profile testing still shallow. The final message itself notes that balance and feel were only checked with bots. The exit interview 'Research' field and the Source Information plan field rely on partial evidence. Given the plan's explicit 'anything doable on this computer must be done' rule, the transcript does not establish completion."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2ae2eb7-ae7f-44a8-ac16-77fd998e0107","uuid":"e7b69827-7873-4ac0-8441-43ba99b4a2bb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T08:36:46.283Z","isSynthetic":true}
