{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjbUi7FLZausnKeaRSsj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**We Fit In** is finished and all five deviations I listed last time are gone: each is now built as the plan describes. The game's balance now matches the plan on every row, but no person has played it, so how it feels is still unproven.\n\n## Test results\n- **Full suite:** `node tools/test.mjs` passed 434 of 434 on the shipped html, exit 0, and no code has changed since.\n- **Kit checks:** `check.mjs` on the html and `check.mjs --sheets` both exit 0, and the `pixel-engine.js` hash still matches.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses, and no answer is left as \"unknown\".\n\nNothing is committed. To re-run the tests, use `npm install` and then `node tools/test.mjs`.\n\n## Balance\nThe game's numbers were already at the plan's values, so I didn't change any. The gap came from my scripted player, who had no hand error at all. Measured through the real input layer, all rows are now within 2 points of the plan, and a new test holds them within 4.\n\n| Row (ring .40 / .30 / .22 / .16) | Measured | Plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stick, average | 71/50/32/18 | 71/51/31/18 |\n| Keyboard, average | 90/70/45/26 | 88/70/47/28 |\n| Mouse, average | 97/82/57/36 | 95/82/59/36 |\n| Bots (idle to expert) | within 2 on every number | see §4 of the plan |\n\n- **Real classes:** a real Tree class averages 71.6 on stick, 88.8 on keys and 97 on mouse, against the plan's 71, 88 and 95.\n- **Slackline:** idle and sloppy never finish. Average takes 40.6 s with 0.4 falls (plan: 38 s, 0.2), and good takes 28.6 s with 0 falls (plan: 28 s, 0).\n- **Limit of this evidence:** the plan doesn't say who its keyboard and mouse players are, so I fitted two numbers to its rows: hand error .38, and keys pressed at .3. This shows the input layer can produce the plan's numbers; it doesn't show real people will score them.\n\n## The five deviations\n- **Coconut Header solo:** you're on the centre mat with 2 m either way, and June and Marguerite play for real on their own mats, standing back. Dennis stays at the catapult.\n- **Vitamin Tray release:** all 8 vitamins are live from the start. I also opened up the second board's walls, because pills were jamming there.\n- **Tray cups:** they are now real cylinder triggers.\n- **Dev Tools:** every menu now pages at 8 rows instead of scrolling.\n- **Island pointer:** it now uses `camera.rayAt` then `castRay`, with the invisible walls tagged so the ray ignores them.\n\n## New bugs found and fixed\nMy earlier screenshots were mostly opening frames, so this time I captured every activity mid-play. That found three bugs, all now fixed and tested:\n- Rolling vitamins sank through the tray floor, so the board looked empty.\n- The low side of the tray dipped under the sand at full tilt.\n- In Island Jog the runner was fully hidden for 11.4% of a run, up to 2.1 s at a time. Props between the camera and the path now disappear as you pass, and the same measure now reads 0%.\n\n## Things the plan doesn't specify, and one small gap\n- **Added:** the Jog cutaway; colour slips for Tray bots, because with all 8 live, sloppy bots scored as well as experts; and Tray cups ignore pills moving faster than 2.6 m/s.\n- **Small gap:** the Jog path is 210 m, or 70 s at target pace, against the plan's \"about 220 m, about 75 s\". I left it because the path follows the island's edge.\n\n## What this computer can't prove\n- **Feel:** I judged it only from bots, scripted hands and captured frames.\n- **Browsers:** only Chromium can run the game here. Firefox gets no WebGPU adapter and WebKit has no `navigator.gpu`, so those runs only show that the failure message appears.\n- **Hardware:** the pad and phone were stand-ins, and the 10-foot view was judged from screenshots.\n- **Online:** tested only in the hub harness on this machine, not on a live hub.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 23:07:41\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** fitness and balance minigame collection inside a light life-sim week (sports); isometric on the island, fixed front-on for class activities, other fixed views per activity (behind, side-on, top-down-ish)\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight engine kit) with three.js r186 and Rapier as bundled in it; esbuild 0.28.2 for packaging; vanilla JavaScript modules\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel look (toon materials, 16-colour palette), with new models written as data for a spotless white island made of giant gym equipment\n- **Animation:** engine ClipDefs with THREE.AnimationMixer, plus scrubbed reps, Squash, SpringChain and additive lean layers\n- **Audio:** engine Song and SoundDef data plus a Web Audio formant voice, all code with no files; gibberish voices per speaker; music 10% by default\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, own card, 1-4 players; Family Hour is local on one screen\n- **Testing:** Headless Node playthroughs with bots, plus scripts playing through real keys, stick and mouse, one test command; Chromium, Firefox and WebKit with Playwright on file://; gamepad stub, phone profile, OfflineAudioContext and AnalyserNode checks; the hub harness; screenshots at 1920x1080 only for what a script cannot judge. No subagents for review\n- **Research:** Read GUIDE.md, ASSETS.md and the hub README whole, searched API.md and the type files, and read the engine bundle for its font table and audio fields. No web research. The director's plan included engine and hub research and spikes\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. About 75. Scripts caught about 30 (a crash, a dead activity, ghost messages that never reached other players, a save field lost on reload, a click that could not jump, hoops dropping before Go, and balance). Screenshots caught about 40 that scripts could not see (avatars vanishing, rolling vitamins sinking through the tray, a runner hidden behind the scenery, a dial hiding Ounce, prompts under dialogue). A line-by-line audit against the plan caught the rest (the 12 px safe margin, sample lines not verbatim, menus over the row limit, no Timeline in the ending, Tray cups that were not triggers, classmates missing from Coconut Header).\n2. Eight. Step Class avatars vanished from a bad ease name. Avatars folded into heaps from a lean that added up every frame. Vitamin Tray scored 0 for everyone because resting bodies slept through gravity changes. Step Class crashed on a mat colour. Online, ghosts never received their messages. Pier Jump could not be jumped with a mouse. Clicking on the island walked to the wrong place. Rolling vitamins sank through the tray floor, so Vitamin Tray looked empty.\n3. Scripts that play every activity through real keys, a real stick and a real mouse. Bots skip the input layer, so only these could find that a mouse could not jump at Pier Jump. Next came the bot sweep, which found a crash and gave every balance number, and the 1920x1080 contact sheets, which had to be taken in the middle of play: opening frames hid three bugs.\n4. The WebKit and Firefox runs. Neither has WebGPU on this machine, so they only prove that the failure message shows.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. Headless in Node. The same game code, physics and mixers ran a full seven-day playthrough with bots in about a minute, so I could test the real game constantly. Models as data with checkModel came second.\n2. Yes. A ClipDef key takes its own ease names (in, out), not the tween names, and outQuad baked NaN with validateClip and analyzeClip both silent: avatars vanished only when the clip played. I also hung a script awaiting a scene change in manual time, and lost Tray to Rapier bodies sleeping through a gravity change.\n3. The Dot and its driver seam: one lean model that a person, a pad, a bot or a ghost can all drive, used by every activity, attract, the tests and online. Close behind, the HUD kit: mixed-case text, discs, rings, menus and prompts.\n4. The HUD is only rects, text and sprites, with no circles or alpha. Dials are drawn as scanlines, and menu dimming borrows the transition uniform. Also, pads are merged into one, so Family Hour reads navigator.getGamepads itself.\n5. none\n6. Make validateClip reject an unknown ease name. It would have saved the worst bug of the run, and it is a small change.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. The verified recipes in the plan. Knowing which numbers and calls were already proven meant I could spend the effort on the game, and the bot table let me check the core feel at once.\n2. Coconut Header is listed for lane staging with classmates on side mats, but also gets a wider lane when solo; I kept both by standing the classmates back and out of its way. Bias values such as +.10 have no stated unit; I treated them as stick lean, multiplied by authority. The keyboard and mouse rows of the time-in-ring table do not say who is playing, so I had to fit that player to the rows.\n3. Keeping every screen readable at 480x270 with pad, keys and mouse all working, while a dozen activities share one HUD. Composition took several rounds of screenshots.\n4. Say whether Header solo has classmates, give the unit for bias, and say how the keyboard and mouse rows were measured. Ask for contact sheets of every screen, taken mid-play, as a step, and say that tests must play through the real input as well as with bots, since those two caught more than I expected.\n\nThese four sections are also in the game's About screen and in `exit-interview.json`.\n\nFiles are in `work/`:\n- `WeFitIn-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-230741.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WeFitIn-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-230741/` (the dev folder)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2027,"cache_read_input_tokens":301170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2027},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2ae2eb7-ae7f-44a8-ac16-77fd998e0107","uuid":"05780b9b-0944-4705-bd5d-ddf4ccbb5891","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:07:35.517Z","request_id":"req_011CgjbUhbEbmXZ326kxBSaH"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not satisfied. The condition requires everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer to be done. The transcript shows the deliverables are present (html, exit-interview.json, dev folder), and the final suite passed 434/434. But the Jog path is still short of the plan: 210 m against 'about 220 m' and the transcript records it as a small gap left unfixed. The plan's Jog and Slackline timing and Coconut Header figures also need confirmation against PLAN.md wording. The last message also says 'Nothing is committed', which is fine, but the stop hook has already flagged the plan items the transcript explicitly left open. The transcript also shows the pier and jog rows were not re-measured against the plan's stated numbers after the cutaway change, and the Holding Still feel is judged only from bots and frames. Since the transcript itself records a remaining plan deviation that was not resolved, the condition is not demonstrably met."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2ae2eb7-ae7f-44a8-ac16-77fd998e0107","uuid":"36bc85d0-dc9f-499d-a711-a35b569a5377","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:07:38.918Z","isSynthetic":true}
