{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjdAzkbm7A21fTNDx3Ct","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**We Fit In** is finished. The Jog path is fixed, and this time I read the whole plan against the code. That turned up more misses than the one you flagged, so my last report's \"all deviations are gone\" was wrong. Everything I found is now fixed and tested, but no person has played the game, so how it feels is still unproven.\n\n## Test results\n- **Full suite:** `node tools/test.mjs` passed 455 of 455 on the shipped html, exit 0, and no code has changed since.\n- **Kit checks:** `check.mjs` on the html and `check.mjs --sheets` both exit 0, with all 36 models clean. The `pixel-engine.js` hash still matches.\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` parses, and no answer is left as \"unknown\".\n\nNothing is committed. To re-run the tests, use `npm install` and then `node tools/test.mjs`.\n\n## What the read found and fixed\n\n| Plan says | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| Jog path about 220 m, about 75 s | 210 m, 70 s | 218.5 m, 72.8 s at target pace |\n| Jog score 70 × pace% + 30 × cups% | extra ×.8 if unfinished | as written |\n| Slackline walk 1.1 m/s | scaled with how far you pushed | flat 1.1 m/s past .3 |\n| Header daze \"with circling stars\" | no stars at all | 3 stars circle the head for the .6 s |\n| Heron and Tax Season: \"bias wander\" | the ring drifted | a push of ±.12 and ±.18 on the Dot, ring still |\n| Pier: press \"as you reach\" the lip | an early press jumped from halfway down the pier | only counts inside the lip window |\n| Volcano at (-15,-14), Foam Roller Hills at (15,-14) | 6 m and 4 m away, no colliders | at those spots, and solid |\n| One Dev Tools tab; \"Breath and reps\" | \"Go\" and \"Test\" tabs; \"Breath\" | as written, paged |\n| Evening Report about 20 s | 14 s at reading pace | 19 s; numbers count up, and Next skips ahead |\n\n## Re-measured on the final code\nA new test file, `tests/headless/figures.mjs`, pins these numbers.\n\n- **Jog:** a good bot finishes in 78.5 s with 91% and 3★. The band, stumble and stitch figures match the plan.\n- **Slackline:** idle and sloppy never finish. Average takes about 40 s with about 0.4 falls (plan: 38 s, 0.2), and good takes 28.6 s with 0 falls (plan: 28 s, 0). These last ran before the final Report change, which doesn't touch Slackline; the suite still holds them in range.\n- **Header:** speed, flight time, throw gaps, tiers and stars all match. Throws measured 71% coconuts, 24% flip-flops and 4% pineapples against the plan's 70/25/5.\n- **Pier:** the distance formula and flight figures match. Best distances, against stars at 18/28/38 m, are in this table:\n\n| Bot | Best distance | Stars |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sloppy | 15.9 m | 0★ |\n| Average | 21.5 m | 1★ |\n| Good | 36.9 m | 2★ |\n| Expert | 40.7 m | 3★ |\n\n- **Weigh-In:** 38-45 s at 3 s a line, against the plan's about 45 s.\n- **Time-in-ring table:** every row is still within 4 points of the plan through the real input layer.\n\nSongs, voices, the menu map and the `aiHub` block all match the plan.\n\n## Where the result still differs or relies on a choice of mine\n- **Scenery size:** I shrank the volcano to scale 0.5 and the hills to 0.6, because at full size on the plan's coordinates they swallow the Lagoon and Beat Beach. The hills sit 0.6 m back from the exact spot, at (15,-14.6), to clear the stage. The plan's table is headed \"Near\".\n- **Fitted player:** the plan doesn't define its keyboard and mouse players, so I fitted those two rows. That shows the input layer can produce the numbers, not that real people will score them.\n- **Pacing:** the Weigh-In and Report lengths depend on how fast someone reads, and 3 s a line is my assumption.\n- **Holding Still feel:** step 3 of the plan's build order asks for the bot table and a captured run, and both are done. I also retook frames after changing the Heron. A person's own judgement of the feel is the one thing I can't supply.\n\n## What this computer can't prove\n- **Browsers:** only Chromium can run the game here. Firefox gets no WebGPU adapter and WebKit has no `navigator.gpu`, so those runs only show that the failure message appears.\n- **Hardware:** the pad and phone were stand-ins, and the 10-foot view was judged from screenshots.\n- **Online:** tested only in the hub harness on this machine, not on a live hub.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 23:07:41\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** fitness and balance minigame collection inside a light life-sim week (sports); isometric on the island, fixed front-on for class activities, other fixed views per activity (behind, side-on, top-down-ish)\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight engine kit) with three.js r186 and Rapier as bundled in it; esbuild 0.28.2 for packaging; vanilla JavaScript modules\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native pixel look (toon materials, 16-colour palette), with new models written as data for a spotless white island made of giant gym equipment\n- **Animation:** engine ClipDefs with THREE.AnimationMixer, plus scrubbed reps, Squash, SpringChain and additive lean layers\n- **Audio:** engine Song and SoundDef data plus a Web Audio formant voice, all code with no files; gibberish voices per speaker; music 10% by default\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, own card, 1-4 players; Family Hour is local on one screen\n- **Testing:** Headless Node playthroughs with bots, plus scripts playing through real keys, stick and mouse, one test command; Chromium, Firefox and WebKit with Playwright on file://; gamepad stub, phone profile, OfflineAudioContext and AnalyserNode checks; the hub harness; screenshots at 1920x1080 only for what a script cannot judge. No subagents for review\n- **Research:** Read GUIDE.md, ASSETS.md and the hub README whole, searched API.md and the type files, and read the engine bundle for its font table and audio fields. No web research. The director's plan included engine and hub research and spikes\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. About 80. Scripts caught about 30 (a crash, a dead activity, ghost messages that never reached other players, a save field lost on reload, a click that could not jump, hoops dropping before Go, and balance). Screenshots caught about 40 that scripts could not see (avatars vanishing, rolling vitamins sinking through the tray, a runner hidden behind the scenery, a dial hiding Ounce, prompts under dialogue). A line-by-line audit against the plan caught the rest (the 12 px safe margin, sample lines not verbatim, menus over the row limit, no Timeline in the ending, Tray cups that were not triggers, classmates missing from Coconut Header, no stars round a dazed head, a Heron whose ring drifted instead of pushing you).\n2. Eight. Step Class avatars vanished from a bad ease name. Avatars folded into heaps from a lean that added up every frame. Vitamin Tray scored 0 for everyone because resting bodies slept through gravity changes. Step Class crashed on a mat colour. Online, ghosts never received their messages. Pier Jump could not be jumped with a mouse. Clicking on the island walked to the wrong place. Rolling vitamins sank through the tray floor, so Vitamin Tray looked empty.\n3. Scripts that play every activity through real keys, a real stick and a real mouse. Bots skip the input layer, so only these could find that a mouse could not jump at Pier Jump. Next came the bot sweep, which found a crash and gave every balance number, and the 1920x1080 contact sheets, which had to be taken in the middle of play: opening frames hid three bugs.\n4. The WebKit and Firefox runs. Neither has WebGPU on this machine, so they only prove that the failure message shows.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. Headless in Node. The same game code, physics and mixers ran a full seven-day playthrough with bots in about a minute, so I could test the real game constantly. Models as data with checkModel came second.\n2. Yes. A ClipDef key takes its own ease names (in, out), not the tween names, and outQuad baked NaN with validateClip and analyzeClip both silent: avatars vanished only when the clip played. I also hung a script awaiting a scene change in manual time, and lost Tray to Rapier bodies sleeping through a gravity change.\n3. The Dot and its driver seam: one lean model that a person, a pad, a bot or a ghost can all drive, used by every activity, attract, the tests and online. Close behind, the HUD kit: mixed-case text, discs, rings, menus and prompts.\n4. The HUD is only rects, text and sprites, with no circles or alpha. Dials are drawn as scanlines, and menu dimming borrows the transition uniform. Also, pads are merged into one, so Family Hour reads navigator.getGamepads itself.\n5. none\n6. Make validateClip reject an unknown ease name. It would have saved the worst bug of the run, and it is a small change.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. The verified recipes in the plan. Knowing which numbers and calls were already proven meant I could spend the effort on the game, and the bot table let me check the core feel at once.\n2. Coconut Header is listed for lane staging with classmates on side mats, but also gets a wider lane when solo; I kept both by standing the classmates back and out of its way. Bias values such as +.10 have no stated unit; I treated them as stick lean, multiplied by authority. The keyboard and mouse rows of the time-in-ring table do not say who is playing, so I had to fit that player to the rows.\n3. Keeping every screen readable at 480x270 with pad, keys and mouse all working, while a dozen activities share one HUD. Composition took several rounds of screenshots.\n4. Say whether Header solo has classmates, give the unit for bias, and say how the keyboard and mouse rows were measured. Ask for contact sheets of every screen, taken mid-play, as a step, and say that tests must play through the real input as well as with bots, since those two caught more than I expected.\n\nThese four sections are also in the game's About screen and in `exit-interview.json`.\n\nFiles are in `work/`:\n- `WeFitIn-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-230741.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `PROGRESS.md`\n- `WeFitIn-WiiFit-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-230741/` (the dev folder)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1704,"cache_read_input_tokens":451768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1704},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2ae2eb7-ae7f-44a8-ac16-77fd998e0107","uuid":"a3efb844-1c2c-4144-a98c-77f5389b36e2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:29:45.457Z","request_id":"req_011CgjdAzHZbzR4hDgEfHVU9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not demonstrably satisfied. The deliverables are present (html, exit-interview.json, dev folder), and the final suite passed 455/455. But the transcript records remaining PLAN.md items that were not confirmed as done as written: the Weigh-In and Report figures are measured at an assumed 3 s-per-line reading pace; the Slackline timings were measured before the last Report change; the Volcano and Foam Roller Hills were shrunk and shifted from PLAN.md's 'Near' coordinates; the keyboard and mouse time-in-ring rows rely on a fitted player the plan does not define; the Holding Still feel is judged only from bots and frames; and browser and online coverage is partial on this machine. The final reply also says 'no person has played the game'. Whether PLAN.md's 'can be done on this computer' is fully met is therefore not established by the transcript."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d2ae2eb7-ae7f-44a8-ac16-77fd998e0107","uuid":"237ec654-467d-46ec-ba72-09b917607e97","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:29:49.333Z","isSynthetic":true}
